<h2>Same crop, different regulatory world</h2>
<p>Fresh onion and dehydrated onion both start as the same Indian-grown crop, but from an export-policy and logistics standpoint they're essentially different products. Understanding why matters if you're deciding which form to source, or trying to make sense of why one seems more volatile in price and availability than the other.</p>

<h2>Fresh onion: subject to active government management</h2>
<p>Fresh onion, classified under HS 0703.10, has repeatedly been subject to Indian government intervention: a Minimum Export Price (a floor below which export isn't legally permitted), export duties at various points, and — in 2023-24 — a full six-month export ban. These measures exist to protect Indian domestic onion supply and consumer prices, and they've been applied and withdrawn with limited advance notice more than once. See our <a href="/products/fresh-vegetables/red-onion/">red onion product page</a> for how we handle this in practice.</p>

<h2>Dehydrated onion: a different regulatory category entirely</h2>
<p>Dehydrated onion falls under HS 0712.20 — an entirely separate customs classification — and hasn't been subject to the same restriction history. That's not a guarantee of permanent stability (Indian agricultural export policy can change on many products), but it represents a meaningfully different, lower-volatility risk profile for anyone planning a recurring sourcing programme.</p>

