<h2>Which should you choose?</h2>
<p>This isn't purely a substitution decision — the two serve different applications. If your business needs fresh onion for produce sale or culinary use requiring fresh texture, dehydrated isn't a substitute regardless of its pricing stability advantages. If you're a food manufacturer using onion as a formulation ingredient — soups, seasoning blends, snack coatings, ready meals — dehydrated onion's shelf life, consistent specification and policy stability make it worth serious consideration even where fresh might seem like the "more natural" default choice.</p>

<h2>A practical middle path: qualify both</h2>
<p>Buyers who use meaningful volumes of onion in any form have an option worth considering even if they don't expect to need it: qualifying a dehydrated supply line alongside a fresh one, even while fresh remains the primary source. Supplier qualification, specification sign-off and inbound quality checks all take time to establish, and doing that groundwork before a policy disruption forces the issue is far less costly than doing it during one. This is a common risk-management pattern among larger food manufacturers sourcing from India, and it's worth discussing with us even if your near-term order is fresh-onion only.</p>
